course: dictionary
lesson: 4654
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The words around it, its context, show you which meaning the writer wants. Hive is a good example. In «When people say bee they often picture a honeybee hive», a hive is a home for bees. But in «The classroom before the party was a hive of activity», nobody is talking about bees at all.
explain: Ask yourself: what is the sentence about? Insects, a party, or a skin problem? In «Swelling, hives (itchy raised bumps on the skin), coughing or trouble breathing», the brackets explain the meaning, so the word means a rash. Read on before you guess.
explain: Then check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. If it fits, you have probably found it. If it does not, try another meaning from the dictionary. Many words have a list of meanings, and the right one is the one that makes sense in this sentence.
fact: A hive is a home for bees: a box made by people, or a natural nest.
fact: Most of the around 20,000 kinds of bee do not live in hives. Most of them live alone in little holes.
fact: «A hive of activity» means a very busy place full of people working.
fact: Hives (plural) can also mean itchy red bumps on the skin, often caused by an allergy.
fact: Context tells you which meaning is right, so look at the words around the word before you decide.
exercise: «A honeybee does a waggle dance in the hive; the angle and length of the dance tell other bees which way and how far to fly for food.» What does hive mean here? | To separate part of something | Itchy red bumps on the skin | A busy place full of people working | *A home for bees
exercise: The classroom before the party was «a hive of activity». What does this mean? | *A very busy place full of people working | Itchy red bumps on the skin | Separated so it can be run on its own | A home for bees
exercise: «Swelling, hives (itchy raised bumps on the skin), coughing or trouble breathing» can be signs of an allergy. Which meaning of hives is used? | A busy party | Splitting something into parts | *Itchy red bumps on the skin | A wooden box for bees
test: In «Most bees live alone in little holes, not in hives», what is a hive? | An itchy rash | *A home for bees, such as a box or a nest | To split something off | A busy place full of people
test: In the UK, to hive off part of something means what? | *To separate part of something so it is run on its own | To sting someone who comes too close | To fly far away to find food | To paint something gold
exam: In which sentence does hive mean all the bees living together in one home? | The kids made a hive of activity in the hall. | The hive was a busy place full of people working. | Hives may be a sign of an allergy. | *The whole hive swarmed out of the box in spring.
